Let $M$ be a compact piecewise-linear $3$-manifold with a finite simplicial triangulation $T$ consisting of $t$ tetrahedra. A **normal arc** in a triangular face is a properly embedded arc whose endpoints lie in the interiors of two distinct edges and which cuts off the third vertex. A **normal disk** in a tetrahedron is a properly embedded disk whose boundary is a union of normal arcs, one on each face it meets, and whose disk type is one of the following: one of four **triangle types**, each cutting off one tetrahedron vertex, or one of three **quadrilateral types**, each separating one pair of opposite vertices from the other pair. Disks of the same type are parallel copies. A **normal surface** is a properly embedded piecewise-linear surface that meets every tetrahedron in a finite disjoint union of these normal disks and whose arcs match across every interior face. The empty surface is admitted for coordinate addition and has the zero vector.

For each of the seven disk types in each tetrahedron, the **normal coordinate vector** $v(S)\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^{7t}$ records the number of disks of that type in a normal surface $S$. For an interior face shared by tetrahedra $\Delta$ and $\Delta'$, and for each of the three normal-arc types $\tau$ in that face, its **face-matching equation** is the equality between the sum of coordinates of disk types in $\Delta$ whose boundary contributes an arc of type $\tau$ and the corresponding sum for $\Delta'$. A disk type contributes either one such arc or none. A nonnegative integer vector is **admissible** when it satisfies all these equations and, in each tetrahedron, at most one of the three quadrilateral coordinates is nonzero. The **Haken cone** $C(T)\subseteq\mathbb R^{7t}$ is the set of nonnegative real vectors satisfying only the face-matching equations; it does not impose quadrilateral restrictions. A nonzero admissible integer vector is **fundamental** if it is not the sum of two nonzero admissible integer vectors. A **primitive vertex vector** is the unique nonnegative integer vector with relatively prime coordinates on a one-dimensional face (extreme ray) of $C(T)$.

A standard schematic records the two disk shapes in a tetrahedron. The vertices are $A,B,C,D$; each red polygon is the intersection of a normal disk with the tetrahedron boundary.

~~~tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9, every node/.style={font=\small}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (2.7,0); \coordinate (C) at (1.35,2.2); \coordinate (D) at (1.45,0.95);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle; \draw (A)--(D)--(B); \draw (C)--(D);
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$}; \node[below right] at (B) {$B$}; \node[above] at (C) {$C$}; \node[right] at (D) {$D$};
\draw[red,thick] (0.39,0.20)--(0.68,0.30)--(0.65,0.45)--(0.43,0.48)--cycle;
\node[red] at (0.30,0.70) {triangle};
\begin{scope}[xshift=4.1cm]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (2.7,0); \coordinate (C) at (1.35,2.2); \coordinate (D) at (1.45,0.95);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle; \draw (A)--(D)--(B); \draw (C)--(D);
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$}; \node[below right] at (B) {$B$}; \node[above] at (C) {$C$}; \node[right] at (D) {$D$};
\draw[red,thick] (0.72,0.14)--(1.98,0.14)--(2.03,0.29)--(0.68,0.30)--cycle;
\node[red] at (1.35,0.62) {quadrilateral};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
~~~

The picture is only a schematic of the boundary intersection pattern. The definition is the stated proper embedding and matching condition; no proof below relies on the drawing.

For every valid connected one-component oriented planar knot code with $n$ crossings together with a specified polygonal realization $P$ whose regular projection has that code, there is an explicit finite combinatorial construction of a triangulated $S^3$ containing that same $P$ as a subcomplex. An abstract valid code can first be realized by the stated reconstruction procedure and then used as $P$. A **good exterior triangulation** is the triangulation inherited on the complement of the interior of a regular neighborhood made from one fixed ball around each vertex of $P$ and one fixed tube block along each edge, with its boundary torus triangulated and a meridian path marked. The construction gives a good exterior triangulation with at most $C(n+1)$ tetrahedra for one universal constant $C$, and the marked meridian is a path of at most $C(n+1)$ boundary edges. At $n=0$ the construction uses a fixed solid-torus exterior triangulation.

A local crossing is lifted by separating the over- and under-strands in the interval direction; the schematic below records only the projected crossing, with the continuous strand passing over the gapped strand.

~~~tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.15]
\draw[very thick] (-1,-1)--(1,1);
\draw[white,line width=7pt] (-1,1)--(1,-1);
\draw[thick] (-1,1)--(-0.18,0.18);
\draw[thick] (0.18,-0.18)--(1,-1);
\node at (0.62,0.78) {over};
\node at (-0.70,0.76) {under};
\end{tikzpicture}
~~~

The picture records only the planar local incidence. The proof constructs disjoint arcs in a three-dimensional crossing block and does not infer embeddedness from the drawing.

For the crossingless oriented unknot code, triangulate its solid-torus exterior $D^2\times S^1$ by triangulating $D^2$ as one triangle with vertices $a_0,a_1,a_2$ and $S^1$ as the three-edge cycle $b_0b_1b_2b_0$. In the prism over $b_0b_1$, write $a_i^0=(a_i,b_0)$ and $a_i^1=(a_i,b_1)$ and use the tetrahedra
$$T_1=[a_0^0,a_1^0,a_2^0,a_2^1],\qquad T_2=[a_0^0,a_1^0,a_1^1,a_2^1],\qquad T_3=[a_0^0,a_0^1,a_1^1,a_2^1].$$
The properly embedded normal disk $D^2\times\{s_*}$ at the midpoint $s_*$ of $b_0b_1$ meets $T_1$ in the triangle cutting off $a_2^1$, $T_2$ in the quadrilateral separating $\{a_0^0,a_1^0\}$ from $\{a_1^1,a_2^1\}$, and $T_3$ in the triangle cutting off $a_0^0$; every other normal coordinate is zero. It is connected, has nonempty boundary and Euler characteristic one, and its boundary meets the marked original meridian $\mu=\{a_0\}\times S^1$ once mod two.

~~~tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
\coordinate (A0) at (0,0); \coordinate (A1) at (2,0); \coordinate (A2) at (1,1.5);
\coordinate (B0) at (0.6,0.7); \coordinate (B1) at (2.6,0.7); \coordinate (B2) at (1.6,2.2);
\draw (A0)--(A1)--(A2)--cycle; \draw (B0)--(B1)--(B2)--cycle;
\draw (A0)--(B0) (A1)--(B1) (A2)--(B2);
\draw[dashed] (A0)--(B1) (A0)--(B2) (A1)--(B2);
\coordinate (M0) at (0.3,0.35); \coordinate (M1) at (2.3,0.35); \coordinate (M2) at (1.3,1.85);
\draw[red,very thick] (M0)--(M1)--(M2)--cycle;
\node[below left] at (A0) {$a_0^0$}; \node[below right] at (A1) {$a_1^0$}; \node[left] at (A2) {$a_2^0$};
\node[left] at (B0) {$a_0^1$}; \node[right] at (B1) {$a_1^1$}; \node[above] at (B2) {$a_2^1$};
\node[red] at (1.35,0.8) {$D^2\times\{s_*\}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
~~~

The dashed edges indicate the staircase prism subdivision specified by $T_1,T_2,T_3$; the red section is the geometric disk whose intersections with those tetrahedra give the stated triangle–quadrilateral–triangle vector. The formula for the tetrahedra, not the schematic projection, fixes the exact triangulation.
